Fix D2 (dashboard contrast) and D7 (duplicate new-tab control)

D2 -- WCAG AA on the dashboard.
The dashboard paints text over a Wikimedia photo behind a gradient scrim, so
there is no fixed background colour and therefore no single contrast ratio.
The honest measure is the WORST case: the same text over the brightest pixel a
photo can supply, with the scrim composited on top. Measured that way, every
text element on the page failed:

  .name-label  1.12:1  (need 3.0 -- 48px large text)
  .name-skip   1.10:1  (need 4.5)
  .settings    1.84:1  (need 4.5)
  .credit      2.36:1  (need 4.5)

On the dark page background they all "passed" (5.29-16.82:1), which is why
this went unnoticed -- a probe that measures the page background rather than
the worst-case photo reports the page as fine.

.settings was the worst offender because `opacity: 0.85` multiplied into an
already-translucent colour (0.6 x 0.85 = 0.51 effective alpha). text-shadow
does not count toward WCAG and could not rescue any of them.

Fixing this by darkening the global .scrim would need alpha ~0.80 and bury the
wallpaper the page exists to show. Instead each affected element gets a LOCAL
backdrop, which bounds the worst case while leaving the photo alone:

  .credit / .settings  pill backdrop 0.55, text 0.92  ->  8.59:1
  .name-form           panel backdrop 0.55            ->  5.17:1 / 4.68:1

Alphas were solved numerically, not chosen by eye; the sweep is recorded in
documentation/VERIFICATION.md D2. Whether the result looks right is still a
human call (BLOCKED.md B7) -- this patch only guarantees the floor.

D7 -- one pref, two contradictory checkboxes.
`zen.urlbar.replace-newtab` was bound by two live controls with OPPOSITE
polarity: kavachaNewtabFloatingSearch in Zen's Looks and Feel pane (direct)
and kavachaAppearanceNewtabDashboard in Kavacha's Appearance pane (inverse).
Ticking either silently unticked the other, and they read as contradicting
each other because one is phrased positively and the other negatively. Both
were Kavacha's own additions (0011 and 0031 respectively), so this is not a
question of overriding upstream.

The Appearance pane keeps it: Kavacha owns that pane, settings consolidation
into Kavacha panes was the 0030-0032 decision, and 0041/0042 settled the
wording there. The Looks and Feel checkbox is removed, with a comment at the
removal site so the next person does not re-add it.

Verified: all 42 preceding patches plus this one apply cleanly at the pin.
--- aa/src/zen/kavacha-newtab/newtab.css
+++ bb/src/zen/kavacha-newtab/newtab.css
@@ -85,14 +85,31 @@ body {
   );
 }
 
+/* D2 (WCAG AA over an arbitrary photo).
+   The .scrim above is a gradient over the wallpaper, so there is no fixed
+   background colour and no single contrast ratio -- the honest measure is the
+   WORST case, the same text over the brightest pixel a photo can supply.
+   Measured there, these two corners were 2.36:1 (.credit) and 1.84:1
+   (.settings) against a 4.5:1 requirement: unreadable over any bright photo.
+   text-shadow does not count toward WCAG and cannot rescue them.
+
+   Darkening the global .scrim enough to fix this needs alpha ~0.80, which
+   would bury the wallpaper. A local backdrop on just these small elements
+   bounds the worst case at 8.59:1 and leaves the photo alone. The sweep
+   behind these alphas is in documentation/VERIFICATION.md D2 -- recompute it
+   before retuning them rather than adjusting by eye. */
 .credit {
   position: fixed;
   bottom: 1rem;
   right: 1.4rem;
   font-size: 0.8rem;
-  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.75);
+  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.92);
   text-decoration: none;
   text-shadow: 0 1px 10px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.6);
+  background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.55);
+  padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
+  border-radius: 999px;
+  backdrop-filter: blur(6px);
   max-width: 44vw;
   overflow: hidden;
   text-overflow: ellipsis;
@@ -109,18 +126,21 @@ body {
   bottom: 1rem;
   left: 1.4rem;
   font-size: 0.8rem;
-  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.6);
+  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.92);
   text-decoration: none;
   text-shadow: 0 1px 10px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.6);
-  opacity: 0.85;
-  transition:
-    color 0.2s,
-    opacity 0.2s;
+  background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.55);
+  padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
+  border-radius: 999px;
+  backdrop-filter: blur(6px);
+  /* the old opacity: 0.85 multiplied INTO the text alpha (0.6 x 0.85 = 0.51),
+     which is what put this furthest under AA of anything on the page.
+     Hover keeps its emphasis through colour alone. */
+  transition: color 0.2s;
 }
 
 .settings:hover {
   color: #fff;
-  opacity: 1;
 }
 
 /* ---- onboarding ---- */
@@ -136,6 +156,15 @@ body {
 .name-form {
   text-align: center;
   width: min(90vw, 640px);
+  /* D2: this prompt sits mid-page, where the .scrim gradient is at its
+     weakest (alpha 0.05). Over a white photo that left the 48px label at
+     1.12:1 and Skip at 1.10:1. A modal backdrop takes them to 5.17:1 and
+     4.68:1; it also reads as a deliberate first-run panel rather than text
+     floating on a photo. */
+  background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.55);
+  padding: 2rem 1.75rem;
+  border-radius: 1.25rem;
+  backdrop-filter: blur(10px);
 }
 
 .name-label {
@@ -168,7 +197,9 @@ body {
   margin-top: 1.6rem;
   background: transparent;
   border: none;
-  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.6);
